Now, you know, the thing is, that if you go back to the original performance, even though it was such a big moment, it wasn't actually a success in terms of winning the contest. The Irish entry didn’t win. They got fourth place. That's right. But it changed everything else. It created this massive interest in Irish dancing globally, and people wanted to learn it, and it inspired a whole new generation of dancers. And that was really the beginning of the modern era of Irish dancing. And it was all because of that seven-minute segment. So, yes, it was a huge cultural moment.

But here's the thing: I don't want anyone to misunderstand me — I'm not claiming credit for inventing Riverdance. I’m not saying I made it happen. What I am saying is that I was present at the beginning. I saw it unfold. I helped shape some early decisions, like casting Flappy and Butler, and I was part of the team that turned it into a full production. But the real magic — the spark — came from the performers themselves, especially Flappy and Butler. Their energy, their charisma, their connection with the audience — that was what made it unforgettable. And I believe that the reason it resonated so deeply is because it felt authentic. There was no pretense, no artifice. It was raw, powerful, and emotionally charged. That authenticity is what people responded to. So while I played a role, I’m not trying to take ownership of the entire story. I’m simply sharing what I witnessed and how I contributed within my capacity.

